  "query_preview": "/Through Friday/...\nIssued at 147 PM MDT Fri Aug 7 2026\n\nCurrent visible satellite imagery shows cumulus developing over the \nmountains, as a slight increase in mid-level moisture might allow a \nfew virga showers for the high terrain this afternoon. With the \nupper level ridge centered over the Desert Southwest, heat will \ncontinue to build this afternoon and through the weekend. Current \nsurface observations show temperatures in the mid/high 80s across \nthe plains, and we will likely be able to reach mid/upper 90s like \nyesterday. Due to west/northwest flow aloft, it looks like smoke \nwill continue to be in the area through the weekend.\n\nOver the weekend, the axis of the mid-level thermal ridge will shift \neastward, with 700-mb temperatures reaching up to 19-21 C. We are \nstill on track for surface temperatures to reach up to the upper \n90s/low 100s both Saturday and Sunday. With expected cloud cover to \nincrease Saturday night, overnight low temperatures will struggle to \ncool down. Guidance even has some areas not reaching below 80 \ndegrees F till 10 PM. For this reason, have decided to expand and \nextend the Heat Advisory to now include the northern I-25 Corridor \nfrom 11 AM Saturday to 8 PM Sunday.\n\nDue to the dry airmass in place, elevated fire weather conditions \nwill persist through the weekend, particularly for the mountains and \nhigh valleys. However, guidance does keep winds marginal (gusts \ngenerally below 25 mph) and thus shy of Red Flag criteria. The only \nexception could be the northern mountains and high valleys (North \nPark) where brief gusts of 25-30 mph is possible Saturday afternoon. \nDue to the localized nature of these gusts, have held off on any \nhighlights. \n\nIn terms of precipitation this weekend, guidance does keep most of \nthe region dry. However, with the slight increase in mid-level \nmoisture, the mountains could see a few afternoon showers. Models do \ntry to resolve a few virga showers spilling off the foothills and \nonto the plains and thus have increased PoPs to show this potential \n(10-15% chance). Modeled Skew-T soundings indicate steep low/mid-\nlevel lapse rates, with DCAPE up to 1500-1800 J/kg, so gusty outflow \nwinds will be the main concern.\n\nTemperatures in the mid/upper 90s will likely continue towards the \nbeginning of next week. Ensemble guidance has the upper level \nridge weakening through the week and eventually shifting more east\ninto New Mexico/Texas (although GFS has this occurring later in \nthe week compared to ECMWF). This will result in \"cooler\" \ntemperatures but it is still unclear when exactly that will occur \nnext week. Right now, NBM has max temperatures of low-to-mid 80s \nby Thursday for the plains, which could occur if the ridge evolves\nhow the ECMWF depicts it to. However, if the high pressure area \nis slower to track east like the GFS depicts, we might not see \nthose \"cooler\" temperatures till later. Regardless, with the \nbreakdown of the ridge, moisture should continue to filter into \nthe region, with increasing widespread precipitation chances for \nthe mountains and plains by the end of next week/weekend. Seems \nlike a promising signal as of now, but will continue to monitor.",
